== TASK (apply-plan step ③): implement public whole-message recv + tests ==

Add two public C functions that receive one whole record (all parts) in a single call into a
caller-provided array. They COEXIST with the existing *_recv_part family (do not remove or change it).

Signatures (add to core/include/zlink/socket/api.h next to the recv_part family, with doc comments):

  ZLINK_EXPORT zlink_recv_result_t zlink_recv (
    void *s_,
    const zlink_routing_id_t **source_rid_out_,
    zlink_msg_t *parts_out_, size_t parts_capacity_, size_t *part_count_out_,
    zlink_recv_flags_t flags_);

  ZLINK_EXPORT zlink_recv_result_t zlink_router_recv (
    void *router_,
    const zlink_routing_id_t **source_rid_out_,
    zlink_reply_token_t *reply_token_out_,
    zlink_msg_t *parts_out_, size_t parts_capacity_, size_t *part_count_out_,
    zlink_recv_flags_t flags_);

  zlink_recv: raw PAIR/DEALER. zlink_router_recv: ROUTER. Other types -> ZLINK_RECV_NOT_SUPPORTED
  (ENOTSUP), matching how zlink_recv_part / zlink_router_recv_part reject unsupported types.

Implementation (reuse the internal whole-record pull — do NOT add a second copy of pull logic):
- Router impl in core/src/api/socket/socket_request_reply_router_api.cpp; PAIR/DEALER impl in
  core/src/api/socket/socket_message_api.cpp. Share validation helpers with the *_recv_part impls.
- The internal whole-record pull already exists and is what recv_part builds on:
    reqrep::recv_router_record(handle, &src_rid, &reply_token, &parts_out, &part_count_out, flags, ...)
      (socket_request_reply_runtime_io.cpp:951, decl socket_request_reply_internal.hpp:540)
    reqrep::recv_dealer_record(handle, &parts_out, &part_count_out, flags, ...)
      (runtime_io.cpp:1186, decl internal.hpp:551)
  These return a Core-owned zlink_msg_t* array + count for one whole record.
- Study how router_recv_part_impl (same file, ~:120) already: checks recv_sequence_active(helper_state),
  pulls a whole record via recv_router_record, returns the first part, and stages the rest with
  stage_router_recv_sequence -> stage_recv_sequence into handle_state.recv (take_recv_part drains one
  at a time). socket_message_api.cpp does the DEALER analog. Reuse these exact building blocks.

Contract to implement (from spec + decisions D63-3/D63-4):
1. Hot path (capacity sufficient): pull one whole record, MOVE its parts into the caller's parts_out_
   slots (each slot becomes a caller-owned zlink_msg_t; caller closes via zlink_multipart_close — slots
   need NOT be pre-initialized), write *part_count_out_, publish source_rid (borrowed view; router also
   reply_token: 0 for DATA, nonzero for REQUEST). PAIR/DEALER fill source_rid_out_ with NULL (optional
   for zlink_recv). NO staging on this path (that is the whole point: bypass the per-part sequence
   cursor/buffer on the common path).
2. Capacity too small (parts_capacity_ < record part count): do NOT lose data and do NOT truncate.
   Stage the entire pulled record (cursor at 0, nothing delivered) using the existing staging path,
   write the needed count to *part_count_out_, and return ZLINK_RECV_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL (ENOBUFS). Other
   outputs unchanged. A later whole-message recv with adequate capacity drains the SAME record exactly
   once; a later *_recv_part also drains it correctly (cursor 0). This mirrors the subscribe_part
   topic-buffer-too-small idiom.
3. If a *_recv_part sequence is already mid-record (some parts already delivered, cursor > 0) when a
   whole-message recv is entered, return ZLINK_RECV_BUSY (EBUSY) — a coherent whole record cannot be
   delivered mid-sequence. (A fully-staged, nothing-delivered record from case 2 is drainable, not
   EBUSY.) Foreign-thread / concurrent entry keeps the existing EBUSY behavior.
4. DONTWAIT: no record -> ZLINK_RECV_NO_DATA (EAGAIN) immediately; a present record returns the whole
   record (never a half record — record atomicity guarantees no partial record exists). NONE: same
   RCVTIMEO / termination / ESHUTDOWN behavior as zlink_recv_part.
5. Failure leaves outputs and message content unchanged. Borrowed source-RID lifetime identical to
   recv_part (valid until the same socket's next data-recv entry or close).
6. NULL required handle/output -> ZLINK_RECV_INVALID_HANDLE (EFAULT). Unknown flags bit ->
   ZLINK_RECV_INVALID_STATE (EINVAL). parts_out_ and part_count_out_ are required; zlink_router_recv
   also requires source_rid_out_ and reply_token_out_; zlink_recv's source_rid_out_ is optional.

